From 114045251fcc23c531b6183e3b34ffa25cf1ab09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ElyasEbrahim <klaymarshmello51@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:12:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [BOLT] Add profile-guided partial inlining of fast paths

Hot functions often consist of a short fast path that returns early (cache
hit, buffer with free space, disabled tracing level, non-empty free list)
and a large slow path. They are too big to be inlined as a whole, cannot be
inlined across translation units without LTO, and are frequently not
shrink-wrapped, so every call also executes the full prologue and epilogue.

Add a new pass, enabled with --partial-inline, that copies the profile-hot,
acyclic fast region of a callee into its hot direct call sites:

* Instructions that only build or tear down the callee's stack frame are
  not copied (SP adjustments, callee-saved register spills and reloads, FP
  setup, return address signing/authentication, CFI, BTI landing pads).
* Callee-saved registers used as temporaries by the fast path are renamed
  to scratch registers that are clobbered by the call or provably dead
  after it (using RegAnalysis, so that callers relying on interprocedural
  register allocation stay correct).
* Returns fall through to the code after the call (or remain returns at
  tail call sites).
* Every edge leaving the region branches to the original call. The region
  is required to be free of side effects and register hazards on every
  path to such a slow exit, so the callee re-executes from its entry with
  the state of the original call and is not modified.

Call sites are selected with a conservative worst-case model for the
context-insensitive profile and transformed hottest first. Block and edge
counts of the caller copies, the slow-path call and the callee are updated.

New MCPlusBuilder hooks: analyzeFrameInstruction() (frame instruction
classification), getCallSiteScratchRegs() and getFullyDefinedReg(). Frame
elision is implemented for AArch64; on X86 only fast paths that do not
touch the stack are copied.

On an Apple M3 Max running Linux/AArch64 the pass speeds up fast-path heavy
microbenchmarks by up to 2.7x over the BOLT baseline, and does not change
the run time of workloads without the pattern.
