new file mode 100644
index 000000000..8eadf5de6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bolt/docs/PartialInlining.md
@@ -0,0 +1,92 @@
+# Partial Inlining of Callee Fast Paths
+
+`--partial-inline` copies the hot *fast path* of a callee into its hot call
+sites. It targets functions that are too large to be inlined as a whole but
+usually return after a few instructions: a cache hit, a buffer with free space,
+a disabled tracing level, a non-empty free list, an early argument check. The
+compiler cannot inline such functions across translation units without LTO,
+and it frequently cannot shrink-wrap them either, so every call pays for the
+call, the return, and the full prologue and epilogue.
+
+## Transformation
+
+For a direct call (or tail call) of function `F` at a hot call site the pass
+computes the *fast region* of `F`: the acyclic set of profile-warm blocks that
+starts at the entry, contains no calls or indirect branches, and ends in
+returns. The region is copied into the caller:
+
+* instructions that only build or tear down `F`'s stack frame are not copied
+  (stack pointer updates, spills and reloads of callee-saved registers, frame
+  pointer setup, return address signing/authentication, CFI, BTI/ENDBR landing
+  pads) - *frame elision*;
+* callee-saved registers that the fast path uses as temporaries are renamed to
+  scratch registers that the call already clobbers, or that are dead after the
+  call in the caller;
+* returns become fall-throughs to the code after the call (they remain returns
+  at tail call sites);
+* every edge that leaves the region (a *slow exit*) is redirected to a block
+  that performs the original call.
+
+```
+  caller:                         caller after partial inlining:
+    ...                             ...
+    bl memo_get        ===>         <copy of memo_get's fast path>  --miss-->  bl memo_get
+    <continue>                      <continue>                                 b <continue>
+```
+
+`F` itself is not modified. On a slow exit the original call re-executes `F`
+from its entry. This is correct because the pass only accepts regions where,
+on every path from the entry to a slow exit,
+
+1. no instruction has memory or other side effects (stores, atomics, barriers,
+   system instructions);
+2. no register is written after being read (or read without being defined
+   first) - the re-executed callee therefore observes exactly the state of the
+   original call and takes the same path;
+3. the scratch registers introduced by renaming are not read by `F` before
+   they are written.
+
+Instructions on paths that can no longer reach a slow exit (after the
+fast/slow decision) may store to memory. Code that accesses the stack, the
+frame pointer or the return address register is never copied.
+
+## Profile model
+
+BOLT profiles are context-insensitive: the probability that `F` takes its fast
+path is known for all calls of `F`, not per call site. With
+`--partial-inline-profile-model=worst-case` (the default) the pass assumes that
+all slow-path invocations of `F` may come from the call site under
+consideration, so a call site with `N` calls takes the fast path with
+probability at least `1 - SlowCalls(F) / N`. Call sites whose estimate is below
+`--partial-inline-min-fast-ratio` (60% by default) are left alone. This keeps
+call sites that only exercise the slow path from paying for the duplicated
+region check. `--partial-inline-profile-model=average` uses the callee-wide
+probability instead.
+
+Only call sites executed at least `--partial-inline-min-count` times (default:
+BOLT's hot threshold) are considered. They are transformed hottest first until
+`--partial-inline-coverage` percent (99 by default) of the eligible fast-path
+calls are covered.
+
+## Usage
+
+```
+llvm-bolt prog -o prog.bolt -data prog.fdata --partial-inline \
+  --reorder-blocks=ext-tsp --reorder-functions=cdsort --split-functions
+```
+
+`-v=1` prints every transformed call site, `-v=2` additionally prints why a
+callee has no usable fast region. Frame elision is implemented for AArch64. On
+x86-64 only fast paths that do not touch the stack (and their `endbr64`
+landing pads) are copied.
+
+## Limitations
+
+* Fast paths that store to memory *before* the fast/slow decision (for
+  example reference count decrements) or that overwrite an argument register
+  before it (`ldr x0, [x0]`) cannot use the re-execution fallback.
+* Fast paths with calls, loops, jump tables, stack accesses (spills, locals,
+  stack arguments) or reads of callee-saved entry values are not copied.
+* The copied instructions lose their debug line information, and the callee
+  no longer appears in stack traces taken on its fast path.
+* Volatile loads on the path to a slow exit are executed twice on that path.
