diff --git a/bolt/lib/Passes/PartialInlining.cpp b/bolt/lib/Passes/PartialInlining.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..4e71d75d2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bolt/lib/Passes/PartialInlining.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,1416 @@
+//===- bolt/Passes/PartialInlining.cpp - Fast-path partial inlining -------===//
+//
+// Part of the LLVM Project, under the Apache License v2.0 with LLVM Exceptions.
+// See https://llvm.org/LICENSE.txt for license information.
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
+//
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+//
+// This file implements the PartialInlining pass. See PartialInlining.h for an
+// overview of the transformation.
+//
+// Correctness argument (re-execution fallback)
+// --------------------------------------------
+// Let R be the region copied from callee F into caller G at the call site S.
+// The copy R' executes in G's context instead of F's. On a path through R'
+// that ends in a return ("fast exit"), R' performs exactly the computation and
+// memory accesses F performs on that path, except for the dropped frame
+// instructions. Those only touch F's own (dead after return) stack frame, the
+// return address register and callee-saved registers whose original values are
+// restored before F returns; callee-saved registers that F uses as temporaries
+// are renamed to scratch registers that the call to F already clobbers.
+//
+// On a path through R' that leaves the region ("slow exit") control reaches
+// the original call. It must observe the state of the original call, so:
+//   (1) no instruction on a path from the entry to a slow exit may have memory
+//       side effects (stores, atomics, barriers, system instructions, ...);
+//   (2) no register may be written on such a path and also read there before
+//       it is (must-)defined: then F's re-execution recomputes identical
+//       values and takes the same path to the same slow exit;
+//   (3) scratch registers introduced by renaming must not be read by F before
+//       they are written (they are caller-saved, non-argument registers that
+//       F's own code never reads first).
+// Under (1)-(3) the re-executed call is indistinguishable from the original
+// one, so F does not need to be modified and other callers are unaffected.
+//
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+
